//@version=6

// ==================================================================
// OBJECTIVE: A Combination of EMA to Draw Cloud Pattern on Chart
//
// The "2-EMA Line System" is one of the oldest and most durable
// frameworks in technical analysis where we plot one "fast" (short)
// and one "slow" (long) exponential moving average line on the same
// chart and their relationships determine the state of the security.
//
// Cloud Theory: Generate a Colored Shaded Cloud based on EMA Pairs
//
// The cloud theory is inspired from "SD Cloud Master Indicator" in
// TradingView. This provides configurable length and EMA lengths to
// create a long-/short-term trading strategy based on the coverage.
//
// Example/Usage(s) of EMA Pairs
//
// EMA Pairs serve different purposes. Here are examples of typical
// pairs that are used in institutions, market research and other
// published strategies:
//
// Scalping/Intraday Momentum [5, 13] - Linda Raschke's Classic Fast
// Pair; Very Reactive. Recommended Timeframe: 5-15 mins. Chart
//
// Short-Term Swing/Intraday Trend [7, 21] - Popular on Crypto and NSE
// (Indian Securities); Catches Multi-Day Momentum.
//
// Short-Term Swing [9, 21] - Slight Variation, Popular on FOREX and
// Futures Desks.
//
// Ripster Pivot Cloud [34, 50] - Ripster's key support/resistance and
// bias filter (bullish over, bearish under); "that 34-50 EMA cloud is
// your risk level" - good as a higher-timeframe swing filter.
//
// Ripster Trend Cloud [72, 89] - Ripster's structural trend cloud;
// 34/89 are Fibonacci numbers (convention, no proven edge on the go).
// Note (I): our default long pair (30, 74) is a slightly detuned hybrid
// of these two Ripster clouds - it blends "pivot" and "trend" rather
// than keeping them distinct (documented, defaults unchanged).
//
// Institutional Gold Standard [50, 200] - The most watched global
// standard. The crossover strategies are (I) "Golden Crossover" when
// the 50 EMA crosses above 200 EMA and (II) "Death Crossover" when
// the 50 EMA falls below the 200 EMA line.
//
// Check https://github.com/growthio/tradingview for details.
// Check https://in.tradingview.com/u/ZenithClown/ for TV Profile.
// ==================================================================

library('cloudTheory', overlay = true)

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Helper: Bounded "squash()" Function, NaN/Overflow-Safe Checks
//
// TanH() is not present in Pine Script v6, so we use the algebraic
// identity tanh(x) = 2 / (1 + exp(-2x)) - 1. The input is clamped to
// [-20, 20] to avoid math.exp() overflow; result is bounded [-1, 1].
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

squash(series float x) =>
    xc = math.max(-20.0, math.min(20.0, x))
    na(x) ? na : 2.0 / (1.0 + math.exp(-2.0 * xc)) - 1.0

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Helper: Baz et al. (2015) CTA Standard Response "bazCTA()" Function
//
// Unlike tanh (which saturates monotonically to ±1), this response
// PEAKS near x = sqrt(2) (~1.41) then attenuates toward 0 for large
// |x| - embedding an over-extension / mean-reversion guard. The 0.89
// divisor is per the paper (Section 3.2, eq. 32). na-safe.
//
// It is recommended to use the `bazCTA()` method instead of the
// bounded `squash()` method since the function tries to modulate the
// value when value is too far from mean; however a lower value does
// not necessarily mean that the signal is bullish/bearish but tends
// to determine that entry is recommended/not at the current level.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

bazResponse(series float y) =>
    na(y) ? na : y * math.exp(-(y * y) / 4.0) / 0.89

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Function Implementation: Draw Shaded Cloud based on EMA Pairs
//
// The function captures the two EMA lengths and based on which it
// determines if the underlying security is in a bullish or bearish
// or neutral phase. The flag and the EMA values can then be used to
// calculate and draw a shaded cloud using a TradingView Indicator.
//
// @param fastLength - Fast EMA Length, the shorter length which
//  typically determines the current rhythm of the security.
//
// @param slowLength - Slow EMA Length, the longer length which
//  determines the underlying drift of the security.
//
// @param src - Which value of the candle should be used to calculate
//  the moving average; defaults to candle close value.
//
// @returns [emaFast, emaSlow, mktSentiment, upCross, dnCross] - EMA
// values and a flag to determine the market sentiment. The sentiment
// flag can be read as: (I) '1' the security is bullish, (II) '-1' the
// security is bearish and (III) '0' denotes neutral. The last two
// values returned are the "EMA Cross" points which can be directly
// used to determine the trend and set an alert.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

export drawCloud(
    simple int fastLength
    , simple int slowLength
    , series float src = close
) =>
    emaFast = ta.ema(src, fastLength)
    emaSlow = ta.ema(src, slowLength)

    // determine the candle zone: bullish/bearish/neutral
    // also, based on the same, drives the cloud color condition
    mktSentiment = (
        src > emaFast and src > emaSlow ? +1 :
        src < emaFast and src < emaSlow ? -1 : 0
    )

    // add the cross over points and return them
    upCross = ta.crossover(emaFast, emaSlow)
    dnCross = ta.crossunder(emaFast, emaSlow)

    [emaFast, emaSlow, mktSentiment, upCross, dnCross]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Function Implementation: Calculation of Price Action on EMA Pairs
//
// The Five Dimensional Framework of Price Action on EMA Pairs is an
// industry-adjacent approach that assigns one single weighted value
// based on the below properties to determine the condition. This is
// used by Trend Following CTAs and Systematic Equity Desks (who run
// it over hundreds of EMA aggregates); we simplify to two EMAs which
// can be extended to multiple pairs on the go (as required).
//
// The helper function takes precomputed EMA values, such that any
// consuming indicators can compute each pair's EMA exactly once (no
// redundant/hanging `ta.ema` calls). The five dimensions are:
//
//  1. Regime/Trend State: position of the fast (shorter) vs the
//      slow (longer) EMA.
//
//  2. Price Location/Zone: determines where the current price sits,
//      relative to the cloud (EMA pairs).
//
//  3. Slope/Momentum: the rate of change of each exponential moving,
//      while largely the same as the ATR units.
//
//  4. Spread/Separation: the distance between the two exponential
//      moving lines → is the spread increasing/decreasing.
//
//  5. Distance from Mean Value: distance from the mean is a standard
//      value which also in turns affects the `z-value` which states
//      the likelihood of value as outlier/not.
//
// All the value normalization is done in ATR units (which provides
// a clean, cross-symbol comparable values) and substitutes the use of
// epsilon garbage values from the spurious `src/emaPrev` factoring.
// NaN value propagation is done, thus the score returns `na` until
// enough bar exists in the terminal.
//
// @param fastLength - Fast EMA Length, the shorter length which
//  typically determines the current rhythm of the security.
//
// @param slowLength - Slow EMA Length, the longer length which
//  determines the underlying drift of the security.
//
// @param src - Which value of the candle should be used to calculate
//  the moving average; defaults to candle close value.
//
// @param atrLength - ATR length for normalization, recommended value
//  is 14 (ATR Length, default) for long-term, and 5 for short term.
//
// Each dimensions are controllable, values are set default as per the
// paper recommendations. TODO: set customization rules.
//
// @param useBaz - Switch between using bazCTA() (default) vs squash()
//  method. The bazCTA() method calculates volatility-normalized
//  response to the pair's MACD while the squash() returns price action.
//
// @returns [regime, zone, fastSlopePct, slowSlopePct, spreadPct,
//  distance, score, isValid, bazScore] - original seven diagnostics
//  plus a warmup validity flag and the per-pair Baz score.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

export priceAction(
    simple int fastLength
    , simple int slowLength
    , series float src = close
    , simple int atrLength = 14
    , simple float wRegime = 0.20
    , simple float wZone = 0.20
    , simple float wFastSlope = 0.20
    , simple float wSlowSlope = 0.10
    , simple float wSpread = 0.15
    , simple float wDistance = 0.15
    , simple bool useBaz = true
) =>
    [emaFast, emaSlow, _, _, _] = drawCloud(
        fastLength, slowLength, src
    )

    // calculate atr, with na-propagation
    atrValue = ta.atr(atrLength)
    safeATR = nz(atrValue) == 0 ? na : atrValue

    // ? (1) Current Regime (fast vs slow EMA)
    regime = emaFast > emaSlow ? 1 : emaFast < emaSlow ? -1 : 0

    // ? (2) Zone relative to the cloud; >= boundaries close the ladder
    //   gap at src == emaFast, with a regime fallback for the middle.
    zone = (
        src >= emaFast and emaFast >= emaSlow ? +2 :
        src <  emaFast and emaFast >= emaSlow ? +1 :
        src >= emaFast and emaFast <  emaSlow ? -1 :
        src <  emaFast and emaFast <  emaSlow ? -2 : regime
    )

    // ? (3) Rate of change of each EMA, in ATR units
    fastSlope = (emaFast - emaFast[1]) / safeATR
    slowSlope = (emaSlow - emaSlow[1]) / safeATR

    // ? (4) Spread / separation of the EMAs, in ATR units
    spread = (emaFast - emaSlow) / safeATR

    // ? (5) Distance of price from the fast EMA, in ATR units
    distance = (src - emaFast) / safeATR

    // ! Per-pair Baz-style pipeline (optional): vol-normalized MACD of
    //   THIS user pair (63-day price stdev, 252-day signal stdev). This
    //   is NOT the canonical 3-scale S_CTA (see bazCTA()).
    macdRaw = emaFast - emaSlow
    priceVol = ta.stdev(src, 63)
    macdNorm = macdRaw / (nz(priceVol) == 0 ? na : priceVol)
    macdVol = ta.stdev(macdNorm, 252)
    macdSignal = macdNorm / (nz(macdVol) == 0 ? na : macdVol)
    bazScore = bazResponse(macdSignal)

    // ! Weighted score of the five dimensions and return
    sRegime = regime
    sZone = zone / 2.0
    sFastSlope = squash(fastSlope)
    sSlowSlope = squash(slowSlope)
    sSpread = squash(spread)
    sDistance = squash(distance / 2.0)

    rawScore = (
        sRegime * wRegime +
        sZone * wZone +
        sFastSlope * wFastSlope +
        sSlowSlope * wSlowSlope +
        sSpread * wSpread +
        sDistance * wDistance
    )

    baseValid = not na(safeATR) and not na(emaFast[1]) and not na(emaSlow[1])
    isValid = useBaz ? (baseValid and not na(bazScore)) : baseValid
    blended = useBaz ? bazScore : rawScore
    score = isValid ? math.max(-1.0, math.min(1.0, blended)) : na

    // backward-compatible percent diagnostics (plain arithmetic)
    fastSlopePct = (emaFast - emaFast[1]) / emaFast[1] * 100
    slowSlopePct = (emaSlow - emaSlow[1]) / emaSlow[1] * 100
    spreadPct = (emaFast - emaSlow) / emaSlow * 100

    [regime, zone, fastSlopePct, slowSlopePct, spreadPct, distance, score, isValid, bazScore]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Function Implementation: Canonical Baz et al. (2015) CTA Signal
//
// This is the actual signal construction used by systematic CTA desks
// (Man AHL / Duke; SSRN 2695101, Section 3.2, eqs. 28-33). It uses
// THREE fixed EWMA time-scale pairs. The paper's numbers S=(8,16,32),
// L=(24,48,96) are EWMA "n" parameters with decay lambda = (n-1)/n,
// i.e. alpha = 1/n - NOT look-back days and NOT half-lives.
//
// Pine's ta.ema uses alpha = 2/(L+1). Setting 2/(L+1) = 1/n gives the
// exact equivalence L = 2n-1, so we use Pine lengths:
//   short 8->15, 16->31, 32->63 ; long 24->47, 48->95, 96->191.
//
// Pipeline per scale k: (I) x_k = EWMA_short - EWMA_long;
// (II) y_k = x_k / RunStDev(price, 63); (III) z_k = y_k /
// RunStDev(y_k, 252); (IV) u_k = R(z_k); (V) S_CTA = mean(u_k).
//
// Warmup: needs ~191 (slowest EWMA) + 63 + 252 bars to fully mature -
// about 2 years of DAILY data. Score is na until valid. Bounded and
// self-attenuating (~[-0.96, 0.96]).
//
// @param src - Which value of the candle should be used to calculate
//  the moving average; defaults to candle close value.
//
// @returns [sCTA, u1, u2, u3, isValid] - Returns the bazCTA values
//  for determining the price action and entry point for the signal.
//  Recommended entry point is when the signal is changing signs
//  (i.e., from negativt → positive: long position and vice versa).
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

export bazCTA(series float src = close) =>
    // ? (I) three vol-normalized MACD scales, 2n-1 converted lengths
    x1 = ta.ema(src, 15) - ta.ema(src, 47)
    x2 = ta.ema(src, 31) - ta.ema(src, 95)
    x3 = ta.ema(src, 63) - ta.ema(src, 191)

    // ? (II) normalize by 63-day realized price volatility (PW = 63)
    priceVol = ta.stdev(src, 63)
    safePV = nz(priceVol) == 0 ? na : priceVol
    y1 = x1 / safePV
    y2 = x2 / safePV
    y3 = x3 / safePV

    // ? (III) normalize each series by its own 252-day stdev (SW = 252)
    v1 = ta.stdev(y1, 252)
    v2 = ta.stdev(y2, 252)
    v3 = ta.stdev(y3, 252)
    z1 = y1 / (nz(v1) == 0 ? na : v1)
    z2 = y2 / (nz(v2) == 0 ? na : v2)
    z3 = y3 / (nz(v3) == 0 ? na : v3)

    // ? (IV) response function per scale
    u1 = bazResponse(z1)
    u2 = bazResponse(z2)
    u3 = bazResponse(z3)

    // ? (V) equal-weight average (w_k = 1/3)
    isValid = not na(u1) and not na(u2) and not na(u3)
    sCTA = isValid ? (u1 + u2 + u3) / 3.0 : na

    [sCTA, u1, u2, u3, isValid]
